A||||15||MickeyAsh||Coolant Fan Motor and Relay - Toasted (twice)||||9||
Z||000000||MickeyAsh||02-08-2001||02:09 AM||||Hey again guys, here's my new problem... a few weeks ago, my coolant fan motor and relay smoked... the relay was totally burnt to a crisp... when I tried to unplug the connector from it, the prongs from the inside of the relay came out with the mishapen plug... There was smoke coming from the fan motor, so I left everything unplugged, ran off to the junkyard, and pulled a relay and fan motor off another fiero... Everything seemed to be working great... fan kicked on exactly when it should... everything was good... then I dropped the car off to be painted a few days later, and just picked it up yesterday... about two miles from the body shop, smoke was coming from the cracks around my headlights, and I knew exactly what was happening... I pulled into a gas station, opened the hood, and there lay my coolant fan and relay smoking and red hot... so I tore the connections I had made to rewire the new relay in last time, trying to save the relay from being burnt beyond use... and here I am... someone suggested that the fan's current is supplied directly from the battery through a fusible link, so the only thing that could cause overcurrent would be the fan getting jammed... is that the only possible reason, or can anyone think of something else to check? Any foresight would be greatly appreciated. Thanks.<P>-Ash||||reg||9
Z||000001||rogergarrison||02-08-2001||08:04 AM||||seems to me youve got a direct short somewhere in the wiring. What was your Alt gauge doing if you noticed it when it fried?||||reg||1
Z||000002||MickeyAsh||02-08-2001||12:25 PM||||I didn't think to look at the batt gage... it would seem to me though that to smoke the motor it would require a shorted HOT wire, to a ground, causing the motor to be hot on both ends... rather than the fan just getting stuck... does that sound plausible?||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||02-08-2001||12:33 PM||||id say thats right. chances of 2 motor and 2 relays bad is a million to one. Use a continuity tester to see if the hot wire is grounding anywhere. If you dont have one, Radio Shack has them for $10. Makes any wiring problems lots easier. They usually have a voltage meter too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||MickeyAsh||02-08-2001||01:52 PM||||I have a digital multimeter that has a continuity mode (buzzer)... how do I go about testing the wiring with it? And another question I have... what wiring do I need to check with it? Just the wiring for the fan circuit, or would I have to go into the junction block and everything else?||||reg||1
Z||000005||MoneypitGT||02-08-2001||05:46 PM||||I had a similar problem happen to me this last year, my original fan died so I replaced it with a brand new fan and relay. I was out cruising when I smelled something and noticed the volts went all they way down to the red, I thought the alt. went but it ended up being the fan. It was only about 2 months old, and brand new, it shorted out completely and took out the relay, all the wires were melted together.  I replaced it all again and has been fine ever since, about a year ago. I took the fan apart and it looked like there was a physical problem, internal short, but there wasn't much left to figure out how. I would just try another, but that is just my .02<BR>  Chris<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fieroproject/moneypitgt.gif">  <BR>2 '87 GT Projects||||reg||3
Z||000006||MoneypitGT||02-08-2001||06:09 PM||||opps<p>[This message has been edited by MoneypitGT (edited 02-10-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000007||theogre||02-08-2001||07:06 PM||||At this point you need to physically disect the harness and inspect all the wires in this circuit. A bad or bound up fan could be a problem but you may also have a partial short in the harness now.<P>you need to replace the relay and it's socket when this kind of thing happens. You also have to make sure all the new connections are weatherproofed.||||reg||1
Z||000008||hugh||02-08-2001||07:15 PM||||A short in the wiring shouldn't cause the problem you describe.If the motor burned both times there are only 2 explanations.<BR>ONE; both motors had problems.<BR>TWO;You may have a low voltage problem,causing the motor to run slower and in turn causing the motor to draw more amps.This translates into heat,especially at connections(relay contacts,brushes if they have them).<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/hugh_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||MickeyAsh||02-09-2001||12:13 AM||||Hmmm... well, I have a new(well, used, but new) junction block off a junkyard fiero, because some wiring on the junction block had gone out a while back and it needed to be replaced,(BTW, I'm talking about the junction block that's by the battery in the engine compartment) and I didnt want to just splice the single wire and risk the chance of it having melted on the inside of the block as well, and shorting something else out... my thoughts are that maybe if I replace that, it would solve the problem... but I'm worried that if the problem isnt in the block or the motor, that I'll have to AGAIN replace the block, the relay AND the motor... which would suck alot... and I still wouldnt know the actual problem... so I think I need a rundown on exactly what I need to do in order to find the problem... any suggestions on that?<p>[This message has been edited by MickeyAsh (edited 02-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||MickeyAsh||02-09-2001||12:20 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by hugh:<BR><B>A short in the wiring shouldn't cause the problem you describe.If the motor burned both times there are only 2 explanations.<BR>ONE; both motors had problems.<BR>TWO;You may have a low voltage problem,causing the motor to run slower and in turn causing the motor to draw more amps.This translates into heat,especially at connections(relay contacts,brushes if they have them).<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That sounds like a good explanation for the motor burning out, but what about the relay?||||reg||1
Z||000011||G-Nasty||02-09-2001||12:35 AM||||Quit buying or replacing the electric motor with ones made in China...Get the ones made in Canada.-those 14 yr old girls in the sweatshops cant make caca...I got a life time warranty from local carparts (O'Reilly).<BR>Hope your engine aint 2 fried.||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||02-09-2001||04:37 AM||||just put one test lead on a bare end of the hot wire, other lead on chassis ground. If it buzzes its shorting somewhere.||||reg||1
Z||000013||hugh||02-09-2001||07:11 AM||||The relay has a moveable contact and if the amperage going through it is high,it can get hot enough to melt.A shorted hot wire will not cause the motor to burn,unless it is a resisted short,meaning,not a dead short and is only bleeding to ground and increasing the electrical load on the circuit lowering the voltage.I am not very familiar with the wiring on the Fieros,but the motor circuit should be fused.If it isn't it needs an inline fuse that is rated higher than the rated amperage of the motor.Hope this helps. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Indiana_resto_guy||02-09-2001||07:42 AM||||Mickey,<P>If you have '84 thru '86 check this out, you may need it.http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/ebayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=552205315&r=0&t=0<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||theogre||02-09-2001||08:40 PM||||If the motor pulled enough power to melt the relay it may also have melted the wiring. This has to be checked out. <P>The wire may have what is often called a "soft" short. The Soft short has some resistance to whatever it's shorted to where a "hard" or "dead" short is a solid zero or very near zero resistance short. Soft shorts can be a giant pain to locate. This type of short can burn the entire harness up and never pop the fuses.<BR>||||reg||1
